Lua, the scripting language, on Zenix (roadmap block 7, its first item; DESIGN D52, docs/d52-self-hosting.md section
9; the user's page is docs/man/lua.txt).

What it is: the reference Lua 5.4 interpreter from lua.org, /bin/lua, with every standard library. Built from this
copy by the Makefile (its "Lua" section) as a plain program (ZEXE), linked with Zenix's C library (picolibc, D27)
and its floating-point printf and scanf.

upstream/  Lua 5.4.9, pinned and never changed: lua.org's lua-5.4.9.tar.gz (2026-08-10, 373,429 bytes, sha256
           2335b6c582a52654f94612bf10d2f4672805d05329aa6568b1d8cd9e5c6fb8e6), its src/*.c and src/*.h (luac.c
           left out: luac is not built); LICENSE is the MIT notice at the end of src/lua.h, word for word (the
           notice that travels with the program: tools/mkroot.sh and the package lua put it in
           /usr/share/licenses/lua).

Zenix's changes: no patch. What Zenix needs is set from outside, by the Makefile's LUA_CFLAGS and by upstream's own
hook for a user header (lua.h includes the file LUA_USER_H names after luaconf.h):
- LUA_USE_POSIX: io.popen (the C library's popen, through /bin/sh -c), os.tmpname (mkstemp), fseeko.
- _setjmp and _longjmp named setjmp and longjmp: picolibc has only those, and they save no signal mask, which is
  what the underscore forms mean.
- LUAI_MAXCCALLS (the Makefile's LUA_MAXCCALLS) and zenix/luauser.h's LUAL_BUFFERSIZE: the C stack is fixed (no
  MMU) and Lua's limit on nested C calls and parser levels is what keeps a script inside it; d52 section 9, "The C
  stack", has the measurements behind the numbers and STACK_lua.
- No dynamic libraries (upstream's default without LUA_USE_DLOPEN): require loads Lua modules only.

Zenix's own module (GPL-2.0-only, not Lua's): zenix/lzenix.c, Lua's "zenix" module for games (a desktop window,
pictures, keys and the mouse, samples and modules through sndd, time; docs/man/zenix-lua.txt, DESIGN D52 section 8
"As built: the fun"). Linked into /bin/lua only (not the probe build) and registered in package.preload by wrapping
luaL_openlibs with the linker's --wrap, so upstream's files stay unchanged; zenix/shim/libz.h lets the display's and
the sound server's client libraries (display/lib/libdisplay.c, user/sndlib.c) and modplay's replayer (user/modrep.c)
compile unchanged against the C library. It adds 23 KB to /bin/lua.

Found while porting (for the record, not changed):
- libgcc's soft float on m68k (lb1sf68) keeps its state in data reached by absolute addresses, so a program using
  doubles cannot be a program with shared code (ZEX2) today: Lua is a plain program, each running Lua has its own
  copy of the code. A pure soft float would make it ZEX2 (d52 section 9).

Tests: tools/lua-host-test.sh (the PC: this copy built with the same settings under AddressSanitizer and UBSan, the
test scripts in rootfs/tests/lua run; its output is the reference) and tools/lua-test.sh (FS-UAE, an A1200 with 4 MB
of fast RAM, in make check: the same script's output compared line for line, every C recursion stopped by Lua's
limit and never by the kernel's stack guard, the command line).

To update: put the new release's src/*.c and src/*.h here (without luac.c), the LICENSE from its lua.h, its name,
size and sha256 above; build; run both tests; read the release's notes for changes to luaconf.h's settings named
above, and bring docs/man/lua.txt and d52 section 9 in step.
